    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">quadratic equation</span> is any equation that can be written in the form <strong>ax&sup2; + bx + c = 0</strong>, where a, b, c are constants and a &ne; 0. In SS1 you met factorization; in Further Mathematics we sharpen every method &mdash; factorization, completing the square, and the quadratic formula &mdash; and learn to read off the <span class="learn-keyword">nature of the roots</span> without fully solving the equation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Solving by Factorization</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Write the equation as a product of two linear brackets equal to zero. <strong>Example:</strong> Solve x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 6 = 0.<br>We need two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to &minus;5: they are &minus;2 and &minus;3.<br>(x &minus; 2)(x &minus; 3) = 0 &rArr; x = 2 or x = 3.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Completing the Square</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Used when factorization is not obvious. <strong>Example:</strong> Solve x&sup2; + 6x + 2 = 0.<br>x&sup2; + 6x = &minus;2 &rArr; (x + 3)&sup2; &minus; 9 = &minus;2 &rArr; (x + 3)&sup2; = 7 &rArr; x = &minus;3 &plusmn; &radic;7.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Quadratic Formula</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">For ax&sup2; + bx + c = 0, x = (&minus;b &plusmn; &radic;(b&sup2; &minus; 4ac)) / 2a. <strong>Example:</strong> Solve 2x&sup2; &minus; 3x &minus; 5 = 0.<br>a = 2, b = &minus;3, c = &minus;5. Discriminant = 9 + 40 = 49. x = (3 &plusmn; 7)/4 &rArr; x = 2.5 or x = &minus;1.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Discriminant and Nature of Roots</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Discriminant &Delta; = b&sup2; &minus; 4ac</th><th>Nature of Roots</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>&Delta; &gt; 0</td><td>Two distinct real roots</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>&Delta; = 0</td><td>One repeated (equal) real root</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>&Delta; &lt; 0</td><td>No real roots (complex/imaginary roots)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Word Problems</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> Two consecutive positive integers have a product of 132. Find them.<br>Let the numbers be n and n + 1: n(n + 1) = 132 &rArr; n&sup2; + n &minus; 132 = 0 &rArr; (n + 12)(n &minus; 11) = 0 &rArr; n = 11 (rejecting n = &minus;12). The numbers are 11 and 12.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> If a question only asks for the <em>nature</em> of the roots, do not solve the whole equation &mdash; just compute &Delta; = b&sup2; &minus; 4ac and use the table above. This saves valuable time in the exam.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Quadratic equations can be solved by factorization, completing the square, or the formula. The discriminant b&sup2; &minus; 4ac tells us the nature of the roots before we even solve. Sum of roots = &minus;b/a; product of roots = c/a.\n</div>\n',
