    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  At any point on a curve, we can draw a straight line that just touches the curve &mdash; this is the <span class="learn-keyword">tangent</span>. The line perpendicular to the tangent at that same point is the <span class="learn-keyword">normal</span>. Both are essential tools for describing the behaviour of curves at a specific point, and they connect coordinate geometry directly to calculus.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Gradient of a Tangent</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The gradient of the tangent to a curve y = f(x) at a point is the value of the derivative dy/dx evaluated at that point. <strong>Example:</strong> For y = x&sup2;, dy/dx = 2x. At x = 3, gradient = 6.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Equation of the Tangent</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Using the point-gradient form y &minus; y&#8321; = m(x &minus; x&#8321;), where m is the gradient at that point. <strong>Example:</strong> Find the tangent to y = x&sup2; at (3, 9).<br>Gradient m = 2(3) = 6. Equation: y &minus; 9 = 6(x &minus; 3) &rArr; y = 6x &minus; 9.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Gradient and Equation of the Normal</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Since the normal is perpendicular to the tangent, its gradient is the negative reciprocal: m<sub>normal</sub> = &minus;1/m<sub>tangent</sub>. <strong>Example:</strong> For the curve above at (3, 9), normal gradient = &minus;1/6. Equation: y &minus; 9 = &minus;(1/6)(x &minus; 3) &rArr; 6y &minus; 54 = &minus;x + 3 &rArr; x + 6y = 57.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Gradient</th><th>Passes through</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Tangent</td><td>m = dy/dx at the point</td><td>The point on the curve</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Normal</td><td>&minus;1/m</td><td>The same point on the curve</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Points Where the Tangent Is Horizontal</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A horizontal tangent occurs where dy/dx = 0. These are the turning points of the curve, which we study in depth under maxima and minima.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always find dy/dx first, substitute the x-value to get the numeric gradient, THEN decide whether you need the tangent (use m directly) or the normal (use &minus;1/m). Mixing these up is the most common error.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The tangent touches the curve with gradient dy/dx at that point; the normal is perpendicular to the tangent with gradient &minus;1/m. Both use the point-gradient equation y &minus; y&#8321; = m(x &minus; x&#8321;).\n</div>\n',
