    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">polynomial</span> is an expression made up of variables and coefficients, involving only addition, subtraction, and non-negative integer powers, such as 3x&sup3; &minus; 2x&sup2; + x &minus; 5. Understanding how to add, subtract, multiply, and divide polynomials is essential before we tackle factor theorem, remainder theorem, and cubic equations later in this term.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Degree and Terms</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The <span class="learn-keyword">degree</span> of a polynomial is the highest power of the variable present. In 4x&#8308; &minus; 3x&sup2; + 7, the degree is 4. The <span class="learn-keyword">leading coefficient</span> is the coefficient of the highest-degree term (here, 4).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Addition and Subtraction</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Combine like terms only. <strong>Example:</strong> (3x&sup3; + 2x&sup2; &minus; x) + (x&sup3; &minus; 5x&sup2; + 4x) = 4x&sup3; &minus; 3x&sup2; + 3x.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Multiplication</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Multiply each term of the first polynomial by every term of the second, then collect like terms. <strong>Example:</strong> (x + 2)(x&sup2; &minus; 3x + 1) = x&sup3; &minus; 3x&sup2; + x + 2x&sup2; &minus; 6x + 2 = x&sup3; &minus; x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 2.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Long Division of Polynomials</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Similar to numerical long division. <strong>Example:</strong> Divide x&sup3; + 2x&sup2; &minus; 5x &minus; 6 by (x &minus; 2).<br>\nx&sup3; &divide; x = x&sup2;: multiply (x &minus; 2) by x&sup2; &rArr; x&sup3; &minus; 2x&sup2;. Subtract: 4x&sup2; &minus; 5x &minus; 6.<br>\n4x&sup2; &divide; x = 4x: multiply (x &minus; 2) by 4x &rArr; 4x&sup2; &minus; 8x. Subtract: 3x &minus; 6.<br>\n3x &divide; x = 3: multiply (x &minus; 2) by 3 &rArr; 3x &minus; 6. Subtract: 0 (no remainder).<br>\nQuotient: x&sup2; + 4x + 3, Remainder: 0.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Addition/Subtraction</td><td>Combine like terms (same power of x)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Multiplication</td><td>Distribute every term, then collect like terms</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Division</td><td>Long division: divide, multiply, subtract, bring down</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> When dividing, always write the polynomial in descending powers first, and insert a placeholder (0x) for any missing power &mdash; e.g., x&sup3; &minus; 1 should be written as x&sup3; + 0x&sup2; + 0x &minus; 1 before dividing.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Polynomials are combined by matching like terms. Multiplication uses full distribution; division uses the repeated divide-multiply-subtract cycle, producing a quotient and possibly a remainder.\n</div>\n',
