    topic: 'Polynomials II — Factor and Remainder Theorem',
    topicCode: 'SS2-FMATH-04',
    module: 'Polynomials',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Instead of always dividing a polynomial in full, the <span class="learn-keyword">Remainder Theorem</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">Factor Theorem</span> let us find remainders and factors instantly by substitution. These two theorems are among the most exam-tested ideas in Further Mathematics.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Remainder Theorem</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If a polynomial f(x) is divided by (x &minus; a), the remainder equals f(a). <strong>Example:</strong> Find the remainder when f(x) = x&sup3; &minus; 4x&sup2; + 2x + 5 is divided by (x &minus; 2).<br>Remainder = f(2) = 8 &minus; 16 + 4 + 5 = 1.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Dividing by (ax &minus; b)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If divided by (ax &minus; b), the remainder is f(b/a). <strong>Example:</strong> Remainder when f(x) = 2x&sup3; &minus; x + 3 is divided by (2x &minus; 1) is f(1/2) = 2(1/8) &minus; 1/2 + 3 = 1/4 &minus; 1/2 + 3 = 2.75.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Factor Theorem</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">(x &minus; a) is a factor of f(x) if and only if f(a) = 0. <strong>Example:</strong> Show that (x &minus; 1) is a factor of f(x) = x&sup3; &minus; 6x&sup2; + 11x &minus; 6.<br>f(1) = 1 &minus; 6 + 11 &minus; 6 = 0. Since f(1) = 0, (x &minus; 1) is a factor.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Finding Unknown Coefficients</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> If (x + 2) is a factor of f(x) = x&sup3; + kx&sup2; &minus; 5x + 6, find k.<br>f(&minus;2) = 0: &minus;8 + 4k + 10 + 6 = 0 &rArr; 4k + 8 = 0 &rArr; k = &minus;2.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Theorem</th><th>Statement</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Remainder Theorem</td><td>Remainder when f(x) &divide; (x &minus; a) is f(a)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Factor Theorem</td><td>(x &minus; a) is a factor of f(x) &hArr; f(a) = 0</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> To test possible factors quickly, try values of x that divide the constant term of f(x) (the Rational Root hint). If f(x) = x&sup3; &minus; 6x&sup2; + 11x &minus; 6, try x = 1, 2, 3, 6 first, since these divide &minus;6.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Remainder Theorem: substitute to find the remainder without dividing. Factor Theorem: if substitution gives zero, that linear expression is a factor. Both save time compared to full long division.\n</div>\n',
