    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Now that we know the Factor and Remainder Theorems, we can use them together to fully <span class="learn-keyword">solve higher-order polynomial equations</span> &mdash; finding every root of a cubic or quartic equation, not just testing one factor.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Full Method</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">\n  <strong>Step 1:</strong> Use trial values (divisors of the constant term) with the Factor Theorem to find one root.<br>\n  <strong>Step 2:</strong> Divide the polynomial by the corresponding linear factor to get a quadratic (or lower) quotient.<br>\n  <strong>Step 3:</strong> Solve the remaining quadratic by factorization or the quadratic formula.\n</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Worked Example</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Solve x&sup3; &minus; 6x&sup2; + 11x &minus; 6 = 0.<br>\nTest x = 1: f(1) = 1 &minus; 6 + 11 &minus; 6 = 0. So (x &minus; 1) is a factor.<br>\nDividing: x&sup3; &minus; 6x&sup2; + 11x &minus; 6 = (x &minus; 1)(x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 6).<br>\nFactorizing the quadratic: x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 6 = (x &minus; 2)(x &minus; 3).<br>\nSo the full factorization is (x &minus; 1)(x &minus; 2)(x &minus; 3) = 0, giving roots x = 1, 2, 3.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Using Sum and Product of Roots (Cubic)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">For ax&sup3; + bx&sup2; + cx + d = 0 with roots &alpha;, &beta;, &gamma;: sum of roots &alpha;+&beta;+&gamma; = &minus;b/a; sum of products of pairs = c/a; product of roots &alpha;&beta;&gamma; = &minus;d/a. These relationships can check your factorization quickly.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1</td><td>Find one root by trial (Factor Theorem)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2</td><td>Divide out the linear factor (long division)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3</td><td>Solve the remaining quadratic</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>4</td><td>State all roots / full factorization</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> After finding all roots, check using sum of roots = &minus;b/a. For x&sup3; &minus; 6x&sup2; + 11x &minus; 6 = 0, sum of roots should be 6, and indeed 1 + 2 + 3 = 6. This is a fast way to catch arithmetic slips.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Combine the Factor Theorem (find one root), polynomial division (reduce to a quadratic), and quadratic methods (find the rest) to solve any factorable cubic equation completely.\n</div>\n',
