    module: 'Polynomials',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Beyond trial-and-error with the Factor Theorem, several standard <span class="learn-keyword">factorization techniques</span> make cubic expressions easier to break down: grouping, sum/difference of cubes, and perfect cube patterns.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Factorization by Grouping</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Group terms in pairs and factor out common factors. <strong>Example:</strong> Factorize x&sup3; + 3x&sup2; &minus; 4x &minus; 12.<br>\nGroup: (x&sup3; + 3x&sup2;) &minus; (4x + 12) = x&sup2;(x + 3) &minus; 4(x + 3) = (x + 3)(x&sup2; &minus; 4) = (x + 3)(x &minus; 2)(x + 2).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Sum and Difference of Two Cubes</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Difference of cubes</td><td>a&sup3; &minus; b&sup3; = (a &minus; b)(a&sup2; + ab + b&sup2;)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sum of cubes</td><td>a&sup3; + b&sup3; = (a + b)(a&sup2; &minus; ab + b&sup2;)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> Factorize x&sup3; &minus; 27. Here a = x, b = 3: x&sup3; &minus; 27 = (x &minus; 3)(x&sup2; + 3x + 9).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Perfect Cube Expansions</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">(a + b)&sup3; = a&sup3; + 3a&sup2;b + 3ab&sup2; + b&sup3;, and (a &minus; b)&sup3; = a&sup3; &minus; 3a&sup2;b + 3ab&sup2; &minus; b&sup3;. Recognizing these patterns saves time. <strong>Example:</strong> x&sup3; + 6x&sup2; + 12x + 8 = (x + 2)&sup3;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Solving Cubic Equations from Factorizations</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Once fully factorized, set each factor to zero. <strong>Example:</strong> (x + 3)(x &minus; 2)(x + 2) = 0 &rArr; x = &minus;3, 2, &minus;2.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Before reaching for the Factor Theorem, always check first whether the cubic can be grouped (4 terms) or matches a sum/difference of cubes pattern (only 2 terms, both perfect cubes) &mdash; these are much faster.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Cubics can often be factorized directly using grouping or the sum/difference of cubes identities, without needing trial substitution. Recognizing the pattern of the expression is the key skill.\n</div>\n',
