    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Mathematical logic</span> studies statements that are either true or false, and how we combine them. This is the foundation for rigorous proof and for computer science. We begin with statements, truth tables, and the three basic connectives: negation, conjunction, and disjunction.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Statements (Propositions)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">statement</span> is a sentence that is definitely true or definitely false, but not both. "Lagos is in Nigeria" is a statement (true). "What time is it?" is NOT a statement &mdash; it cannot be classified as true or false.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Negation (NOT, ~p)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The negation of a statement p, written ~p, reverses its truth value.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>p</th><th>~p</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody><tr><td>True (T)</td><td>False (F)</td></tr><tr><td>False (F)</td><td>True (T)</td></tr></tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Conjunction (AND, p &and; q)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">p &and; q is true only when BOTH p and q are true.</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>p</th><th>q</th><th>p &and; q</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>T</td><td>T</td><td>T</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>T</td><td>F</td><td>F</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>F</td><td>T</td><td>F</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>F</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Disjunction (OR, p &or; q)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">p &or; q is true when AT LEAST ONE of p or q is true (this is the "inclusive or").</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>p</th><th>q</th><th>p &or; q</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>T</td><td>T</td><td>T</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>T</td><td>F</td><td>T</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>F</td><td>T</td><td>T</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>F</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: AND (&and;) needs BOTH true to be true; OR (&or;) only needs ONE true to be true; only ALL false makes OR false. Build truth tables systematically, row by row, listing every combination of T/F.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Statements are true-or-false sentences. Negation flips truth value. Conjunction (AND) needs both true. Disjunction (OR) needs at least one true. Truth tables show every possible combination systematically.\n</div>\n',
