    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Trigonometric functions relate the angles of a right-angled triangle to the ratios of its sides. In Further Mathematics, we extend these ratios beyond acute angles using the <span class="learn-keyword">unit circle</span> and use them to solve problems involving elevation and depression.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Basic Ratios</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Formula (right triangle)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>sin &theta;</td><td>Opposite / Hypotenuse</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>cos &theta;</td><td>Adjacent / Hypotenuse</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>tan &theta;</td><td>Opposite / Adjacent = sin&theta;/cos&theta;</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Special Angles</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>&theta;</th><th>sin &theta;</th><th>cos &theta;</th><th>tan &theta;</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>0&deg;</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>30&deg;</td><td>1/2</td><td>&radic;3/2</td><td>1/&radic;3</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>45&deg;</td><td>1/&radic;2</td><td>1/&radic;2</td><td>1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>60&deg;</td><td>&radic;3/2</td><td>1/2</td><td>&radic;3</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>90&deg;</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>undefined</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Angles of Elevation and Depression</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The <span class="learn-keyword">angle of elevation</span> is measured upward from the horizontal to an object above; the <span class="learn-keyword">angle of depression</span> is measured downward from the horizontal to an object below. <strong>Example:</strong> From a point 50 m from the base of a tower, the angle of elevation to the top is 30&deg;. Find the height.<br>tan 30&deg; = height/50 &rArr; height = 50 &times; (1/&radic;3) &asymp; 28.87 m.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The CAST Rule (Signs in Each Quadrant)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">For angles beyond 90&deg;, the CAST rule tells us which ratios are positive: Quadrant I &mdash; All positive; Quadrant II &mdash; only Sin positive; Quadrant III &mdash; only Tan positive; Quadrant IV &mdash; only Cos positive.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Memorize the special angle table (0&deg;, 30&deg;, 45&deg;, 60&deg;, 90&deg;) exactly &mdash; these appear constantly without a calculator. Use SOHCAHTOA to recall the ratio definitions instantly.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The three basic trig ratios (sine, cosine, tangent) relate triangle sides to angles. Special angle values must be memorized. Elevation/depression problems use these ratios directly; the CAST rule extends the ratios to all four quadrants.\n</div>\n',
