    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The sine, cosine, and tangent functions produce distinctive repeating <span class="learn-keyword">wave-like graphs</span>. Understanding their shape, period, amplitude, and transformations is essential for solving trigonometric equations and modelling periodic phenomena.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Graph of y = sin x</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Starts at (0,0), rises to a maximum of 1 at x = 90&deg;, back to 0 at 180&deg;, down to a minimum of &minus;1 at 270&deg;, back to 0 at 360&deg;. <span class="learn-keyword">Period</span> = 360&deg;. <span class="learn-keyword">Amplitude</span> = 1.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Graph of y = cos x</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Starts at maximum (0, 1), falls to 0 at 90&deg;, to minimum &minus;1 at 180&deg;, back to 0 at 270&deg;, back to maximum at 360&deg;. Period = 360&deg;, Amplitude = 1. Cos x is simply sin x shifted 90&deg; to the left.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Graph of y = tan x</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Unlike sine and cosine, tan x is NOT bounded &mdash; it has vertical asymptotes at x = 90&deg;, 270&deg;, etc. (where cos x = 0). Period = 180&deg; (half that of sine/cosine).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Transformations</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>y = a sin x</td><td>Amplitude becomes a (graph stretched vertically)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>y = sin(bx)</td><td>Period becomes 360&deg;/b (graph compressed horizontally if b &gt; 1)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>y = sin x + c</td><td>Graph shifted up by c units</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>y = sin(x &minus; d)</td><td>Graph shifted right by d degrees (phase shift)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> To sketch quickly, plot the five key points at 0&deg;, 90&deg;, 180&deg;, 270&deg;, 360&deg; for sine/cosine using their known values, then join with a smooth curve &mdash; never with straight lines.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Sine and cosine are smooth waves with period 360&deg; and amplitude 1; tangent repeats every 180&deg; and has vertical asymptotes. Coefficients a, b, c, d in y = a sin(bx + ...) + c control amplitude, period, vertical shift, and phase shift respectively.\n</div>\n',
