    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  We close out trigonometry for the term with the fundamental <span class="learn-keyword">identities</span> that connect sin, cos, and tan, the graphs of the <span class="learn-keyword">inverse trigonometric functions</span>, and a review of the <span class="learn-keyword">Pythagoras theorem</span> that underlies them all.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Basic Identities</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Statement</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Quotient identity</td><td>tan &theta; = sin &theta; / cos &theta;</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pythagorean identity</td><td>sin&sup2;&theta; + cos&sup2;&theta; = 1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Derived identity</td><td>1 + tan&sup2;&theta; = sec&sup2;&theta;</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Using Identities to Simplify Expressions</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> Simplify sin&sup2;&theta; / (1 &minus; cos&sup2;&theta;).<br>Since 1 &minus; cos&sup2;&theta; = sin&sup2;&theta; (from the Pythagorean identity), the expression simplifies to sin&sup2;&theta;/sin&sup2;&theta; = 1.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Pythagoras\' Theorem in Trigonometry</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">In a right triangle, hypotenuse&sup2; = opposite&sup2; + adjacent&sup2;. <strong>Example:</strong> If sin &theta; = 3/5, find cos &theta; (&theta; acute).<br>Using sin&sup2;&theta; + cos&sup2;&theta; = 1: cos&sup2;&theta; = 1 &minus; 9/25 = 16/25 &rArr; cos &theta; = 4/5.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Inverse Trigonometric Functions</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">sin&#8315;&sup1;x (also written arcsin) gives the angle whose sine is x. <strong>Example:</strong> sin&#8315;&sup1;(0.5) = 30&deg;. The domain of sin&#8315;&sup1;x and cos&#8315;&sup1;x is restricted to [&minus;1, 1] since sine and cosine never exceed these bounds; their graphs are the reflection of y = sin x and y = cos x (restricted) in the line y = x.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Whenever you see sin&sup2;&theta; + cos&sup2;&theta;, or 1 &minus; sin&sup2;&theta;, or 1 &minus; cos&sup2;&theta; in a question, immediately think of the Pythagorean identity &mdash; it is the single most tested identity in Further Mathematics trigonometry.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> sin&sup2;&theta; + cos&sup2;&theta; = 1 is the master identity, from which others are derived. Pythagoras\' theorem is its geometric origin. Inverse trig functions "undo" the trig ratios, restricted to valid domains and ranges.\n</div>\n',
