    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Differentiating from first principles every time is slow. Instead, we use standard <span class="learn-keyword">differentiation rules</span>: the power rule, constant rule, and sum/difference rule &mdash; the everyday toolkit for differentiating algebraic functions quickly.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Power Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If y = x&#8319;, then dy/dx = nx&#8319;&#8315;&sup1;. <strong>Example:</strong> y = x&#8308; &rArr; dy/dx = 4x&sup3;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Constant Multiple Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If y = c&middot;f(x), then dy/dx = c&middot;f&prime;(x). <strong>Example:</strong> y = 5x&sup3; &rArr; dy/dx = 5(3x&sup2;) = 15x&sup2;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Constant Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If y = c (a constant with no x), then dy/dx = 0. <strong>Example:</strong> y = 7 &rArr; dy/dx = 0.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Sum/Difference Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Differentiate each term separately, then add/subtract. <strong>Example:</strong> y = 3x&sup2; &minus; 5x + 2 &rArr; dy/dx = 6x &minus; 5 + 0 = 6x &minus; 5.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Derivative</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>x&#8319;</td><td>nx&#8319;&#8315;&sup1;</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>c (constant)</td><td>0</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>c&middot;f(x)</td><td>c&middot;f&prime;(x)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>1/x&#8319; = x&#8315;&#8319;</td><td>&minus;nx&#8315;&#8319;&#8315;&sup1;</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>&radic;x = x&#189;</td><td>&#189;x&#8315;&#189;</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Rewriting Before Differentiating</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Convert roots and fractions to index (power) form first. <strong>Example:</strong> y = 3/x&sup2; = 3x&#8315;&sup2; &rArr; dy/dx = &minus;6x&#8315;&sup3; = &minus;6/x&sup3;.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always rewrite square roots as fractional powers and fractions as negative powers BEFORE applying the power rule &mdash; e.g., &#8731;x = x&#185;&#8260;&sup3;, 1/x&#8309; = x&#8315;&#8309;. Trying to differentiate roots or fractions directly is a common source of mistakes.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The power rule (bring the power down, subtract 1) handles most algebraic terms. Constants vanish upon differentiation. Sums and differences differentiate term-by-term. Always simplify expressions into pure power form first.\n</div>\n',
