    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  When functions are multiplied, divided, or nested inside one another, the basic power rule alone is not enough. We need three more powerful rules: the <span class="learn-keyword">product rule</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">quotient rule</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">chain rule</span>.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Product Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If y = u&middot;v (where u and v are both functions of x), then dy/dx = u&prime;v + uv&prime;.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Differentiate y = x&sup2;(x + 1). Let u = x&sup2; (u&prime; = 2x), v = x + 1 (v&prime; = 1).<br>\ndy/dx = 2x(x+1) + x&sup2;(1) = 2x&sup2; + 2x + x&sup2; = 3x&sup2; + 2x.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Quotient Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If y = u/v, then dy/dx = (u&prime;v &minus; uv&prime;) / v&sup2;.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Differentiate y = x/(x+1). Let u = x (u&prime;=1), v = x+1 (v&prime;=1).<br>\ndy/dx = [1(x+1) &minus; x(1)] / (x+1)&sup2; = [x+1&minus;x]/(x+1)&sup2; = 1/(x+1)&sup2;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Chain Rule</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If y = f(g(x)), then dy/dx = f&prime;(g(x))&middot;g&prime;(x) &mdash; "differentiate the outside, multiply by the derivative of the inside".<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Differentiate y = (3x + 2)&#8308;. Outer function: ( )&#8308;, inner: 3x+2.<br>\ndy/dx = 4(3x+2)&sup3; &times; 3 = 12(3x+2)&sup3;.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Formula</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Product</td><td>u&prime;v + uv&prime;</td><td>Two functions multiplied</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Quotient</td><td>(u&prime;v &minus; uv&prime;)/v&sup2;</td><td>One function divided by another</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chain</td><td>f&prime;(g(x))&middot;g&prime;(x)</td><td>A function nested inside another (composite)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> For the quotient rule, the order in the numerator matters &mdash; it\'s u&prime;v &minus; uv&prime;, NOT uv&prime; &minus; u&prime;v. Getting the sign backwards is the single most common mistake with this rule.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Product rule for multiplied functions, quotient rule for divided functions, chain rule for nested (composite) functions. Identify the structure of the expression first, then choose the matching rule.\n</div>\n',
