    topic: 'Application of Differentiation (Rate of Change, Velocity, Acceleration)',
    topicCode: 'SS2-FMATH-16',
    module: 'Calculus',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Differentiation isn\'t just an algebraic exercise &mdash; it measures <span class="learn-keyword">rates of change</span>. In physics and everyday problems, this gives us velocity, acceleration, and other rates such as how fast a quantity grows or shrinks.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Rate of Change</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If y = f(x), then dy/dx gives the rate at which y changes with respect to x. <strong>Example:</strong> If the area of a circle A = &pi;r&sup2;, then dA/dr = 2&pi;r gives the rate of change of area with respect to radius.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Velocity and Acceleration</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">If s(t) is displacement as a function of time t: <br>\nVelocity v = ds/dt (first derivative of displacement).<br>\nAcceleration a = dv/dt = d&sup2;s/dt&sup2; (derivative of velocity, or second derivative of displacement).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Worked Example</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A particle moves so that its displacement is s = t&sup3; &minus; 6t&sup2; + 9t (in metres, t in seconds).<br>\nVelocity: v = ds/dt = 3t&sup2; &minus; 12t + 9.<br>\nAcceleration: a = dv/dt = 6t &minus; 12.<br>\nAt t = 2: v = 3(4) &minus; 24 + 9 = &minus;3 m/s; a = 12 &minus; 12 = 0 m/s&sup2;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Connected Rates of Change</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When two quantities are related and both change with time, use the chain rule: dy/dt = (dy/dx)&times;(dx/dt). <strong>Example:</strong> If the radius of a circle increases at 2 cm/s, find the rate of increase of area when r = 5 cm.<br>\ndA/dr = 2&pi;r = 10&pi;. dA/dt = dA/dr &times; dr/dt = 10&pi; &times; 2 = 20&pi; cm&sup2;/s.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> When velocity = 0, the particle is momentarily at rest (a turning point in displacement). When velocity is negative, the particle moves in the negative direction. Always state units in your final answer.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Differentiation gives instantaneous rates of change. In kinematics, velocity is the derivative of displacement, and acceleration is the derivative of velocity (second derivative of displacement). Connected rates use the chain rule to relate two changing quantities.\n</div>\n',
