      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Turning points</span> &mdash; the peaks and valleys of a curve &mdash; are where the gradient (first derivative) equals zero. Using the second derivative, we can classify these as maximum or minimum points, essential for optimization problems.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Finding Turning Points</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Set dy/dx = 0 and solve for x. Each solution gives the x-coordinate of a turning point.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Second Derivative Test</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Second Derivative</th><th>Conclusion</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>d&sup2;y/dx&sup2; &gt; 0</td><td>Minimum point (curve is concave up, like a valley)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>d&sup2;y/dx&sup2; &lt; 0</td><td>Maximum point (curve is concave down, like a peak)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>d&sup2;y/dx&sup2; = 0</td><td>Inconclusive &mdash; check further (point of inflection possible)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Worked Example</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Find and classify the turning points of y = x&sup3; &minus; 3x&sup2; &minus; 9x + 5.<br>\ndy/dx = 3x&sup2; &minus; 6x &minus; 9 = 0 &rArr; 3(x&sup2; &minus; 2x &minus; 3) = 0 &rArr; 3(x&minus;3)(x+1) = 0 &rArr; x = 3 or x = &minus;1.<br>\nd&sup2;y/dx&sup2; = 6x &minus; 6.<br>\nAt x = 3: d&sup2;y/dx&sup2; = 12 &gt; 0 &rArr; minimum. y = 27&minus;27&minus;27+5 = &minus;22.<br>\nAt x = &minus;1: d&sup2;y/dx&sup2; = &minus;12 &lt; 0 &rArr; maximum. y = &minus;1&minus;3+9+5 = 10.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Optimization Problems</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> A farmer has 40 m of fencing to enclose a rectangular field against a wall (one side doesn\'t need fencing). Maximize the area.<br>\nLet width = x, length = 40 &minus; 2x. Area A = x(40&minus;2x) = 40x &minus; 2x&sup2;.<br>\ndA/dx = 40 &minus; 4x = 0 &rArr; x = 10. Maximum area = 10(20) = 200 m&sup2;.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always classify EVERY turning point using the second derivative test &mdash; don\'t assume. In optimization problems, define one variable in terms of the other using the given constraint before differentiating.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Turning points occur where dy/dx = 0. The sign of d&sup2;y/dx&sup2; at that point tells us whether it\'s a maximum (negative) or minimum (positive). This technique is the basis of all optimization problems.\n</div>\n',
