    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">circle</span> is the set of all points equidistant from a fixed centre. In coordinate geometry, we describe circles using algebraic equations, allowing us to find centres, radii, and tangent lines using algebra instead of just compasses.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Standard Equation of a Circle</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A circle with centre (a, b) and radius r has equation: (x &minus; a)&sup2; + (y &minus; b)&sup2; = r&sup2;.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Circle with centre (2, 3) and radius 5: (x&minus;2)&sup2; + (y&minus;3)&sup2; = 25.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The General Equation of a Circle</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">x&sup2; + y&sup2; + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0, where centre = (&minus;g, &minus;f) and radius = &radic;(g&sup2; + f&sup2; &minus; c).<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Find the centre and radius of x&sup2; + y&sup2; &minus; 4x + 6y &minus; 12 = 0.<br>\n2g = &minus;4 &rArr; g = &minus;2; 2f = 6 &rArr; f = 3; c = &minus;12.<br>\nCentre = (2, &minus;3). Radius = &radic;(4 + 9 + 12) = &radic;25 = 5.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Equation of a Tangent to a Circle</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The tangent to the circle x&sup2;+y&sup2;=r&sup2; at point (x&#8321;, y&#8321;) on the circle is xx&#8321; + yy&#8321; = r&sup2;. For a general circle, first find the gradient of the radius to the point, then use the perpendicular gradient for the tangent.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Length of Tangent from an External Point</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">From point (x&#8321;, y&#8321;) to circle x&sup2;+y&sup2;+2gx+2fy+c=0, tangent length = &radic;(x&#8321;&sup2;+y&#8321;&sup2;+2gx&#8321;+2fy&#8321;+c).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> To convert the general form to standard form, complete the square separately for the x-terms and y-terms. Always double-check by expanding the standard form back out.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Standard form (x&minus;a)&sup2;+(y&minus;b)&sup2;=r&sup2; directly shows centre and radius. General form x&sup2;+y&sup2;+2gx+2fy+c=0 requires the formulas centre=(&minus;g,&minus;f), radius=&radic;(g&sup2;+f&sup2;&minus;c).\n</div>\n',
