    topic: 'Conic Sections II — The Ellipse',
    topicCode: 'SS2-FMATH-20',
    module: 'Coordinate Geometry',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  An <span class="learn-keyword">ellipse</span> is the locus of points where the SUM of distances to two fixed points (the <span class="learn-keyword">foci</span>) is constant. Planetary orbits, whispering galleries, and rugby balls are all elliptical in shape.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Standard Equation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Centred at the origin: x&sup2;/a&sup2; + y&sup2;/b&sup2; = 1, where a &gt; b &gt; 0 (major axis along the x-axis).<br>\nVertices: (&plusmn;a, 0). Co-vertices: (0, &plusmn;b). Foci: (&plusmn;c, 0), where c&sup2; = a&sup2; &minus; b&sup2;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Key Features</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Major axis length</td><td>2a (along x-axis if a &gt; b)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Minor axis length</td><td>2b</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Focal distance</td><td>c&sup2; = a&sup2; &minus; b&sup2;</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Eccentricity</td><td>e = c/a (0 &lt; e &lt; 1 for an ellipse)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Worked Example</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Find the foci and eccentricity of x&sup2;/25 + y&sup2;/16 = 1.<br>\na&sup2;=25 &rArr; a=5. b&sup2;=16 &rArr; b=4. c&sup2;=a&sup2;&minus;b&sup2;=25&minus;16=9 &rArr; c=3.<br>\nFoci: (&plusmn;3, 0). Eccentricity: e = c/a = 3/5 = 0.6.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Definition Check</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">For any point P on the ellipse, PF&#8321; + PF&#8322; = 2a (sum of distances to both foci is constant, equal to the length of the major axis).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always identify which denominator is larger (a&sup2; or b&sup2;) FIRST &mdash; the larger one tells you which axis (x or y) is the major axis, and this determines whether foci lie on the x-axis or y-axis.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> x&sup2;/a&sup2;+y&sup2;/b&sup2;=1 with a&gt;b describes an ellipse with major axis along the x-axis, foci at (&plusmn;c,0) where c&sup2;=a&sup2;&minus;b&sup2;. Eccentricity e=c/a measures how "stretched" the ellipse is (0 = circle, closer to 1 = more elongated).\n</div>\n',
