    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Probability</span> theory begins with defining the <span class="learn-keyword">sample space</span> (all possible outcomes) and <span class="learn-keyword">events</span> (subsets of outcomes we\'re interested in). This term we lay the foundation; compound events follow next term.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Sample Space</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The sample space S is the set of ALL possible outcomes of an experiment. <strong>Example:</strong> Tossing a coin: S = {Head, Tail}. Rolling a die: S = {1,2,3,4,5,6}.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Events</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">An event is any subset of the sample space. <strong>Example:</strong> "Rolling an even number" is the event E = {2, 4, 6}, a subset of S = {1,2,3,4,5,6}.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Probability Formula</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">P(E) = n(E)/n(S), where n(E) is the number of favourable outcomes and n(S) is the total number of possible outcomes.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> P(rolling an even number) = 3/6 = 1/2.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Types of Events</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Simple event</td><td>Contains exactly one outcome</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Compound event</td><td>Contains more than one outcome</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Certain event</td><td>Always occurs, P = 1 (equals the whole sample space)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Impossible event</td><td>Never occurs, P = 0 (the empty set)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Complementary event</td><td>Event E does NOT occur, P(E&prime;) = 1 &minus; P(E)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always list the sample space carefully before counting outcomes &mdash; especially with two dice or two coins, where the sample space has more elements than students often expect (e.g., two dice give 36 outcomes, not 12).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The sample space lists every possible outcome; an event is a chosen subset of it. P(E) = n(E)/n(S). Probabilities always lie between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain), and P(E) + P(E&prime;) = 1.\n</div>\n',
