      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Building on sample spaces, we now combine events using AND/OR logic. <span class="learn-keyword">Compound events</span> involve two or more simple events, and require the Addition Rule and Multiplication Rule to calculate correctly.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Mutually Exclusive Events (Addition Rule)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Events that cannot happen at the same time (no overlap). P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Rolling a die, P(2 or 5) = 1/6 + 1/6 = 2/6 = 1/3.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Non-Mutually Exclusive Events</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When events CAN overlap: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) &minus; P(A and B).<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Drawing a card, P(King or Heart) = 4/52 + 13/52 &minus; 1/52 = 16/52 = 4/13 (subtracting the King of Hearts, counted twice).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Independent Events (Multiplication Rule)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Events where one does not affect the other: P(A and B) = P(A) &times; P(B).<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Tossing a coin and rolling a die: P(Head and 6) = 1/2 &times; 1/6 = 1/12.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Dependent Events (Conditional Probability)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When the outcome of one event affects the other, P(A and B) = P(A) &times; P(B|A), where P(B|A) is the probability of B given A has occurred.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Drawing 2 cards without replacement, P(2 Kings) = 4/52 &times; 3/51 = 12/2652 = 1/221.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Event Type</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mutually exclusive (OR)</td><td>P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Not mutually exclusive (OR)</td><td>P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) &minus; P(A and B)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Independent (AND)</td><td>P(A and B) = P(A) &times; P(B)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dependent (AND, without replacement)</td><td>P(A and B) = P(A) &times; P(B|A)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> "OR" usually means addition; "AND" usually means multiplication. Always check first whether events are mutually exclusive (can\'t overlap) or independent (don\'t affect each other) before choosing your formula.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Use addition for OR events (subtracting overlap if not mutually exclusive), and multiplication for AND events (adjusting for dependence if outcomes affect each other, especially "without replacement" scenarios).\n</div>\n',
