    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Combinatorics</span> counts the number of ways to arrange or select objects. We start with <span class="learn-keyword">factorials</span> and the <span class="learn-keyword">permutation</span> &mdash; arrangements where ORDER matters.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Factorial Notation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">n! = n &times; (n&minus;1) &times; (n&minus;2) &times; ... &times; 2 &times; 1. By convention, 0! = 1.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> 5! = 5&times;4&times;3&times;2&times;1 = 120.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Permutations (Order Matters)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The number of ways to arrange r objects from n distinct objects: &#8319;P&#8348; = n!/(n&minus;r)!<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Arrange 3 people from a group of 5 in a row. &#8309;P&#8323; = 5!/2! = 120/2 = 60.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Permutations of All n Objects</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Arranging ALL n distinct objects: &#8319;P&#8319; = n!. <strong>Example:</strong> Arrange all 4 letters of "GOLD": 4! = 24 ways.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Permutations with Repetition</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When some items repeat, divide by the factorial of each repeat count. <strong>Example:</strong> Arrange the letters of "LEVEL" (5 letters, with L repeated twice and E repeated twice): 5!/(2!&times;2!) = 120/4 = 30.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Formula</th><th>Use Case</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>&#8319;P&#8348; = n!/(n&minus;r)!</td><td>Arrange r objects from n distinct objects, order matters</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>n!</td><td>Arrange all n distinct objects</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>n!/(p!&middot;q!&middot;...)</td><td>Arrange n objects with repeated items (p, q, ... repeats)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The keyword "arrange" or "order" (like a queue, a race position, a password) signals a PERMUTATION. If swapping two chosen items would create a genuinely different outcome, order matters &mdash; use permutations.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Permutations count arrangements where order matters. &#8319;P&#8348;=n!/(n&minus;r)! for selecting and arranging r from n. Divide by repeated-item factorials when some objects are identical.\n</div>\n',
