    topic: 'Permutation and Combination II',
    topicCode: 'SS2-FMATH-25',
    module: 'Combinatorics',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Now we turn to <span class="learn-keyword">combinations</span> &mdash; selections where ORDER does NOT matter. We also handle circular arrangements and mixed problems that combine both permutations and combinations.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Combinations (Order Doesn\'t Matter)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The number of ways to select r objects from n distinct objects, without regard to order: &#8319;C&#8348; = n!/[r!(n&minus;r)!]<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Choose 3 people from 5 for a committee (no distinct roles). &#8309;C&#8323; = 5!/(3!2!) = 120/12 = 10.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Permutation vs. Combination</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Permutation</th><th>Combination</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Order MATTERS (arrangement)</td><td>Order does NOT matter (selection)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>e.g., race positions, passwords</td><td>e.g., committees, teams, lottery numbers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>&#8319;P&#8348; = n!/(n&minus;r)!</td><td>&#8319;C&#8348; = n!/[r!(n&minus;r)!] = &#8319;P&#8348;/r!</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Circular Permutations</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Arranging n distinct objects in a CIRCLE: (n&minus;1)! ways (since rotations of the same arrangement are considered identical).<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Seat 5 people around a round table: (5&minus;1)! = 4! = 24 ways.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Combined Problems</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> From a group of 6 men and 4 women, select a committee of 3 men and 2 women.<br>\nWays = &#8310;C&#8323; &times; &#8308;C&#8322; = 20 &times; 6 = 120.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Ask yourself: "Does swapping two selected items create a new outcome?" If YES (e.g., 1st vs 2nd place), use permutations. If NO (e.g., both are just "on the committee"), use combinations.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Combinations count selections where order is irrelevant: &#8319;C&#8348;=n!/[r!(n&minus;r)!]. Circular permutations use (n&minus;1)! since rotations don\'t create new arrangements. Combined problems multiply separate combination counts together.\n</div>\n',
