    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Dynamics</span> is the study of forces and their effect on motion. We apply <span class="learn-keyword">Newton\'s Laws of Motion</span> to solve problems involving objects on flat surfaces and inclined planes, always taking g = 10 m/s&sup2; unless stated otherwise.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Newton\'s Three Laws</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Law</th><th>Statement</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1st Law</td><td>An object stays at rest or in uniform motion unless acted on by a net force</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2nd Law</td><td>F = ma (net force = mass &times; acceleration)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3rd Law</td><td>Every action has an equal and opposite reaction</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Motion on a Horizontal Surface</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> A 5 kg block is pushed with a force of 20 N on a frictionless surface. Find its acceleration.<br>\nF = ma &rArr; 20 = 5a &rArr; a = 4 m/s&sup2;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Motion with Friction</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Frictional force F<sub>f</sub> = &mu;R, where &mu; is the coefficient of friction and R is the normal reaction.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A 10 kg block on a horizontal surface (&mu;=0.2) is pushed with 40 N. Find acceleration.<br>\nR = mg = 100 N. F<sub>f</sub> = 0.2&times;100 = 20 N. Net force = 40&minus;20 = 20 N. a = F/m = 20/10 = 2 m/s&sup2;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Motion on an Inclined Plane</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">For a smooth (frictionless) incline at angle &theta;: component of weight along the incline = mg sin&theta;; perpendicular component (normal reaction) = mg cos&theta;.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A 2 kg block slides down a smooth 30&deg; incline. Find its acceleration.<br>\na = g sin&theta; = 10 &times; sin30&deg; = 10 &times; 0.5 = 5 m/s&sup2;.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always draw a clear force diagram first, resolving weight into components along and perpendicular to the direction of motion. This prevents sign errors when several forces act together.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Newton\'s Second Law F=ma is the master equation. On inclines, resolve weight into mg sin&theta; (along the slope) and mg cos&theta; (perpendicular, giving the normal reaction). Friction opposes motion, with magnitude &mu;R.\n</div>\n',
