    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Work</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">power</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">energy</span> describe how forces transfer and transform capacity to do things. These concepts, alongside the work-energy theorem, let us solve motion problems without needing full kinematics equations.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Work Done</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">W = Fd cos&theta;, where F is the applied force, d is the displacement, and &theta; is the angle between force and displacement direction. If force and motion are in the same direction (&theta;=0&deg;): W = Fd.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A force of 50 N moves an object 10 m in the direction of the force. W = 50 &times; 10 = 500 J.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Kinetic and Potential Energy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Kinetic Energy: KE = &frac12;mv&sup2;. Potential Energy (gravitational): PE = mgh.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A 2 kg object moving at 5 m/s: KE = &frac12;(2)(25) = 25 J.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Work-Energy Theorem</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The net work done on an object equals its change in kinetic energy: W = &Delta;KE = &frac12;mv&sup2; &minus; &frac12;mu&sup2;.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A 3 kg object speeds up from 2 m/s to 6 m/s. Find the work done.<br>\nW = &frac12;(3)(36) &minus; &frac12;(3)(4) = 54 &minus; 6 = 48 J.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Power</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Power P = W/t = Work done per unit time (measured in Watts). Also, P = Fv (force &times; velocity, for constant force).<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A motor does 600 J of work in 5 seconds. Power = 600/5 = 120 W.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Formula</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Work</td><td>W = Fd cos&theta;</td><td>Joules (J)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Kinetic Energy</td><td>KE = &frac12;mv&sup2;</td><td>Joules (J)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Potential Energy</td><td>PE = mgh</td><td>Joules (J)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Power</td><td>P = W/t = Fv</td><td>Watts (W)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Energy is always CONSERVED in a closed system without friction: total mechanical energy (KE + PE) stays constant. This lets you solve many problems without finding acceleration or time at all.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Work = Force &times; distance (along direction of motion). KE=&frac12;mv&sup2;, PE=mgh. The work-energy theorem links work done directly to the change in kinetic energy. Power is the rate of doing work.\n</div>\n',
