    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Projectile motion</span> combines constant horizontal velocity with constant vertical acceleration due to gravity, creating the characteristic parabolic path of a thrown or launched object. We analyze the horizontal and vertical components SEPARATELY.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Key Assumptions</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">No air resistance. Horizontal velocity is CONSTANT throughout the flight. Vertical motion has constant acceleration g (downward), so vertical velocity changes uniformly.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Resolving Initial Velocity</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">For initial speed U at angle &theta; above horizontal:<br>\nHorizontal component: U<sub>x</sub> = U cos&theta;. Vertical component: U<sub>y</sub> = U sin&theta;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Key Formulas</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Time to reach maximum height</td><td>t = U sin&theta; / g</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Maximum height</td><td>H = (U sin&theta;)&sup2; / 2g</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Time of flight (returns to launch height)</td><td>T = 2U sin&theta; / g</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Range (horizontal distance)</td><td>R = U&sup2; sin(2&theta;) / g</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Worked Example</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A ball is projected at 20 m/s at 30&deg; above horizontal (g=10 m/s&sup2;). Find the maximum height and range.<br>\nU<sub>y</sub>=20sin30&deg;=10. H = 10&sup2;/(2&times;10) = 100/20 = 5 m.<br>\nR = 20&sup2;&times;sin60&deg;/10 = 400&times;0.866/10 = 34.64 m.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Range is MAXIMIZED when &theta;=45&deg; (since sin90&deg;=1 is the maximum possible value of sin2&theta;). Also remember: at the moment of maximum height, vertical velocity is exactly ZERO (though horizontal velocity is unchanged).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Split initial velocity into horizontal (Ucos&theta;, constant) and vertical (Usin&theta;, decelerating under gravity) components. Use standard formulas for time of flight, maximum height, and range &mdash; each derived from basic equations of motion applied separately to each direction.\n</div>\n',
