    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The <span class="learn-keyword">Binomial Theorem</span> gives a fast way to expand expressions like (a+b)&#8319; without multiplying it out term by term, using combinations to generate the coefficients.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Binomial Theorem</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">(a+b)&#8319; = &#8319;C&#8320;a&#8319;b&#8304; + &#8319;C&#8321;a&#8319;&#8315;&sup1;b&sup1; + &#8319;C&#8322;a&#8319;&#8315;&sup2;b&sup2; + ... + &#8319;C&#8319;a&#8304;b&#8319;<br>\nThe general term: T<sub>r+1</sub> = &#8319;C&#8348;a&#8319;&#8315;&#8348;b&#8348;.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Pascal\'s Triangle</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The coefficients of (a+b)&#8319; can also be read from row n of Pascal\'s Triangle:</p>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>n</th><th>Coefficients</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>1</td><td>1, 1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2</td><td>1, 2, 1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3</td><td>1, 3, 3, 1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>4</td><td>1, 4, 6, 4, 1</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Worked Example</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Expand (x+2)&#8308;.<br>\n= &#8308;C&#8320;x&#8308; + &#8308;C&#8321;x&sup3;(2) + &#8308;C&#8322;x&sup2;(4) + &#8308;C&#8323;x(8) + &#8308;C&#8308;(16)<br>\n= x&#8308; + 4(2)x&sup3; + 6(4)x&sup2; + 4(8)x + 16<br>\n= x&#8308; + 8x&sup3; + 24x&sup2; + 32x + 16.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Finding a Specific Term</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> Find the term in x&sup3; in the expansion of (x+3)&#8308;&#8309;&#8320;.<br>\nGeneral term: T<sub>r+1</sub> = &#8319;C&#8348;x&#8319;&#8315;&#8348;3&#8348;. We need n&minus;r=3.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The powers of a and b always add up to n in every term, and the coefficients &#8319;C&#8348; are symmetric (matching Pascal\'s Triangle). Double-check your expansion has exactly n+1 terms.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> (a+b)&#8319; expands into n+1 terms using binomial coefficients &#8319;C&#8348;. Powers of a decrease from n to 0 while powers of b increase from 0 to n. Pascal\'s Triangle offers a quick way to find small coefficients.\n</div>\n',
