    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Vectors</span> describe quantities with both magnitude AND direction &mdash; essential for representing forces, velocities, and displacements in mechanics. We use column vectors and i-j notation to combine and resolve these quantities.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Vector Notation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A vector can be written as a column (x, y) or using unit vectors: v = xi + yj, where i and j are unit vectors along the x and y axes.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A force of 3i + 4j (N) means 3 N in the x-direction and 4 N in the y-direction.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Magnitude of a Vector</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">|v| = &radic;(x&sup2; + y&sup2;) for v = xi + yj.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> |3i + 4j| = &radic;(9+16) = &radic;25 = 5.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Adding Vectors (Resultant Force)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Add corresponding components. <strong>Example:</strong> Forces F&#8321;=(2i+3j)N and F&#8322;=(5i&minus;j)N act on an object. Find the resultant.<br>\nF<sub>resultant</sub> = (2+5)i + (3&minus;1)j = 7i + 2j (N).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Velocity and Displacement Vectors</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Velocity v = displacement/time (as a vector). <strong>Example:</strong> An object moves from position (1,2) to (5,7) in 4 seconds. Displacement = (4,5). Velocity = (4/4, 5/4) = (1, 1.25) m/s.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Addition</td><td>Add corresponding i and j components</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Magnitude</td><td>|v| = &radic;(x&sup2;+y&sup2;)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Direction (angle)</td><td>&theta; = tan&#8315;&sup1;(y/x)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always add vectors component-by-component (i\'s with i\'s, j\'s with j\'s) &mdash; never combine the magnitudes directly unless the vectors point in exactly the same or opposite directions.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Vectors combine magnitude and direction, written as xi+yj. Magnitude uses Pythagoras: |v|=&radic;(x&sup2;+y&sup2;). Resultant forces or displacements are found by adding vectors component-wise.\n</div>\n',
