    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Integration</span> is the reverse process of differentiation &mdash; given a derivative (gradient function), we find the original function. This is also called <span class="learn-keyword">anti-differentiation</span>, and introduces the "+C" constant of integration.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Power Rule for Integration</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">&#8747;x&#8319; dx = x&#8319;&#8314;&sup1;/(n+1) + C (for n &ne; &minus;1). <strong>Example:</strong> &#8747;x&sup3; dx = x&#8308;/4 + C.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Why "+C"?</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Since the derivative of ANY constant is zero, when reversing differentiation we cannot know what constant was originally present. We always add "+C" (the constant of integration) to indicate this uncertainty, unless given additional information to find its exact value.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Integrating Sums and Constant Multiples</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Integrate term by term. <strong>Example:</strong> &#8747;(3x&sup2; &minus; 4x + 5) dx = x&sup3; &minus; 2x&sup2; + 5x + C.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Finding the Constant C</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Example:</strong> Given dy/dx = 2x + 3 and y = 5 when x = 1, find y.<br>\ny = &#8747;(2x+3)dx = x&sup2;+3x+C. Substituting: 5 = 1+3+C &rArr; C=1. So y = x&sup2;+3x+1.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Integral</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>x&#8319;</td><td>x&#8319;&#8314;&sup1;/(n+1) + C</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>k (constant)</td><td>kx + C</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>1/x&#8319; = x&#8315;&#8319;</td><td>x&#8315;&#8319;&#8314;&sup1;/(&minus;n+1) + C (for n&ne;1)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always ADD 1 to the power and then DIVIDE by the new power &mdash; this is the exact reverse of differentiation\'s "bring down and subtract 1". Never forget the +C unless the question involves definite limits.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Integration reverses differentiation: &#8747;x&#8319;dx = x&#8319;&#8314;&sup1;/(n+1)+C. Always include the constant of integration C, and use given conditions (a known point on the curve) to solve for its exact value when required.\n</div>\n',
