    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  We complete Further Mathematics with <span class="learn-keyword">definite integrals</span> (which give exact numerical answers) and their real applications: finding the <span class="learn-keyword">area under a curve</span> and solving kinematics problems by integrating acceleration and velocity.</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Definite Integral</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">&#8747;<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx = F(b) &minus; F(a), where F(x) is any antiderivative of f(x). No "+C" is needed since it cancels out.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> &#8747;<sub>1</sub><sup>3</sup> x&sup2; dx = [x&sup3;/3]&#8321;&sup3; = 27/3 &minus; 1/3 = 9 &minus; 0.333 = 8.667.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Area Under a Curve</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The area between y=f(x), the x-axis, and the lines x=a, x=b is given by &#8747;<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx (assuming f(x) &ge; 0 in this interval).<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> Find the area under y=x&sup2; between x=0 and x=2.<br>\nArea = &#8747;&#8320;&sup2; x&sup2; dx = [x&sup3;/3]&#8320;&sup2; = 8/3 &minus; 0 = 8/3 square units.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Area Between Two Curves</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Area = &#8747;<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> [f(x) &minus; g(x)] dx, where f(x) is the upper curve and g(x) the lower curve, between their intersection points a and b.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Kinematics via Integration</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Since a=dv/dt and v=ds/dt, we can integrate to reverse the process: v = &#8747;a dt, and s = &#8747;v dt.<br>\n<strong>Example:</strong> A particle has acceleration a=6t. If v=0 at t=0, find v at t=3.<br>\nv = &#8747;6t dt = 3t&sup2; + C. At t=0, v=0: C=0. So v=3t&sup2;. At t=3: v=27 m/s.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#128161;</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> For definite integrals, ALWAYS substitute the upper limit first, then subtract the result of substituting the lower limit &mdash; F(b) &minus; F(a), never the other way round.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">&#127919;</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Definite integrals give exact numerical values by evaluating the antiderivative at the limits and subtracting. This computes areas under curves directly. In kinematics, integrating acceleration gives velocity, and integrating velocity gives displacement.\n</div>\n',
